+## Current State and Unresolved Work
 
-Two approaches remain credible:
+A visit has at most one current revision. When two or more accepted revisions share the same base revision, the visit carries unresolved review work: the set of those competing complete revisions and their contributing submission IDs. Retrieval distinguishes a usable current state from unresolved review work and reports the competing revisions.
 
-- Whole-visit revisions: each edit names its starting revision. Concurrent edits produce competing complete revisions for a coordinator to resolve. This preserves the worker's submitted visit as a unit and needs relatively little merge logic, but even edits to unrelated fields create review work. Tablet-sync would retain base revision IDs and display pending competing revisions. Review-export would need a revision comparison workflow and exclude unresolved visits from its export set.
-- Field-aware three-way merge: each edit supplies its base state and changes. Disjoint field changes combine automatically; overlapping changes require review. This reduces review volume and gets more visits ready for export, but requires maintained rules for scalar fields, observer lists, and species entries. Automatically combined fields can also cross scientific relationships the rules do not understand. Tablet-sync would retain base snapshots and send structured changes. Review-export would need field-level provenance and a review view for overlapping changes.
+While review work is unresolved, retrieval reports the visit as unresolved rather than presenting a single authoritative current state. The server does not pick a winner by arrival order, author, or payload.
 
-The choice trades review workload and submission size against implementation burden and preservation of whole-visit intent. Neither approach may discard original submissions. Field-aware merging is not ruled out by a requirement that every combined record receive manual approval.
+## Resolution Operation
 
-## Downstream Contract Work
+A coordinator resolves review work by selecting one of the competing complete revisions as the visit's current revision. The record store retains the coordinator's identity, the selected revision ID, and the contributing submission IDs with the resolution outcome. All original submissions remain in history. Resolution does not synthesize a new visit or combine fields from multiple revisions.
 
-After selection, define the submission envelope, current-state response, unresolved-work representation, and resolution operation here. Tablet-sync will size its local storage and retry state machine around that envelope. Review-export will size its coordinator interface and export eligibility rules around the unresolved-work representation. Both must link back to this node rather than invent their own concurrency semantics.
+## Downstream Consumers
+
+Tablet-sync (not yet designed) will retain base revision IDs in local drafts, submit complete visit payloads against a base revision, and display pending competing revisions to field workers. Review-export (not yet designed) will present a revision comparison to coordinators and exclude visits carrying unresolved review work from the reviewed CSV. Both are obligations that follow from this node's contract; neither behavior is defined here, and both designs are deferred until this node is complete.
+
+## Decisions & Alternatives
+
+| Decision | Chosen | Alternatives Considered | Rationale |
+|----------|--------|------------------------|-----------|
+| Concurrency mechanism for independent edits to one visit | Whole-visit revisions: store each submission as an intact competing revision and let a coordinator select the current one | Field-aware three-way merge (combine disjoint field changes automatically, review overlaps); last-arrival-wins overwrites | Field-aware merging reduces review volume but requires maintained merge rules for scalar fields, observer lists, and species entries, and can combine edits that are scientifically related without a reviewer noticing. A larger review queue is preferred over that risk, so even unrelated-field edits create review work. Whole-visit revisions preserve each submitted visit as the unit of scientific intent, keep the path from submission to export inspectable, and need no field-combination logic. Last-arrival-wins would silently discard a submitted edit and is excluded by the HLD tenet against discarding submissions. |
+
+## Open Questions & Future Decisions
+
+### Deferred
+1. How the server treats a submission naming an unknown, stale, or already-superseded base revision ID.
+2. Whether a coordinator may resolve by authoring a new synthesized revision rather than selecting one of the submitted revisions.
+3. Behavior when a submission arrives during or after resolution of the same visit's earlier review work.
+4. The API error shape and status codes for rejected submissions (stale base, payload/key mismatch, invalid plot reference).
+5. Idempotency-key scope (per worker, per visit, or global) and collision behavior across workers.
+6. Whether a visit with more than two competing revisions is resolved by a single selection or a sequence.
